WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:07.380
Usually, when we think about the concept of a rule or a constraint, we tend to think of it as something that stocks us.

00:07.660 --> 00:08.820
Right. Like a cage.

00:09.020 --> 00:19.140
Yeah, exactly like a cage. You know, you want to run wild, you want infinite possibilities, but the rule steps in and says, no, stop right there. You can't cross this line.

00:19.140 --> 00:25.700
We inherently view restrictions as the enemy of freedom. I mean, open-ended spaces just feel like pure potential.

00:26.000 --> 00:29.420
Right. Like a blank canvas. It just feels like art waiting to happen.

00:29.420 --> 00:35.000
Exactly. But then you step into the world of mathematics, and suddenly that cage is actually a skeleton.

00:35.140 --> 00:35.800
A skeleton.

00:36.020 --> 00:41.600
Yeah. It's the only reason the thing can stand up at all. What looks like a limitation is, well, it's actually the birthplace of all structure.

00:41.840 --> 00:52.160
And today, we're doing a deep dive into a text that makes this argument just brilliantly. It's called Trigonometry and Transformation, and it's by the Flyxion Independent Researcher.

00:52.240 --> 00:54.020
It's a really fascinating piece of work.

00:54.020 --> 01:04.940
It really is. And the core mission of this deep dive for you listening today is to completely reframe how we view rules, geometry, and specifically, Trigonometry.

01:04.940 --> 01:09.620
Because usually, Trig just triggers traumatic flashbacks to high school exams.

01:09.860 --> 01:22.920
Totally. Just rote memorization of, like, endless formulas. But this text strips all of that away. It presents the subject as a profound philosophical investigation into how constraints create structure.

01:23.400 --> 01:24.940
And how we find invariance.

01:24.940 --> 01:30.260
Yes, covariance. Which is, you know, the things that survive when everything else in the universe is changing.

01:30.720 --> 01:40.460
The author is really challenging you to stop looking at math as just a collection of isolated facts or tools to solve a test question.

01:40.640 --> 01:45.720
Right. Because mathematical understanding is completely transformed when we actually see the network of relationships.

01:45.720 --> 01:55.560
Yeah, we are tracking how incredibly simple, finite rules build up layer by layer to eventually describe the continuous dynamic motion of reality.

01:55.800 --> 02:01.560
So to start us off, the text introduces this really provocative concept, discrete state spaces.

02:01.900 --> 02:04.980
Which, I mean, on the surface, that doesn't sound like trigonometry at all.

02:05.080 --> 02:08.280
No, not at all. It sounds like computer science or something.

02:08.280 --> 02:17.080
It does. But to understand continuous geometry, the author argues we first need to understand finite, discrete symbols.

02:17.440 --> 02:18.260
Okay. Right.

02:18.320 --> 02:23.240
So imagine you have a finite set of symbols, like an alphabet. It could be A through Z or just, you know, zero and one.

02:23.420 --> 02:23.620
Yeah.

02:23.720 --> 02:32.620
If you can string those symbols together in any order whatsoever, without limitation, you don't actually have a structure. You just have chaos.

02:32.720 --> 02:38.000
It's an undifferentiated pile of sequences. Every sequence is as valid as any other.

02:38.300 --> 02:38.780
Exactly.

02:38.980 --> 02:44.900
I was thinking about this, and it's kind of like setting up a password system where the rule is just type anything.

02:45.080 --> 02:45.540
Right, right.

02:45.560 --> 02:47.700
You can just smash your hands on the keyboard and it's accepted.

02:47.700 --> 02:59.040
But the moment you add a constraint, say, like, it must contain a number, and no consecutive identical letters, you immediately partition that infinite chaos into two distinct universes.

02:59.340 --> 03:01.640
The admissible sequences and the inadmissible ones.

03:01.640 --> 03:04.000
Yes. The constraint acts as a filter.

03:04.000 --> 03:07.820
And the text really emphasizes that these constraints are often local.

03:08.220 --> 03:12.820
So whether a symbol is allowed depends entirely on the symbol immediately preceding it.

03:13.080 --> 03:15.680
Okay. So it doesn't care about the whole history of the sequence.

03:15.800 --> 03:17.620
No, not at all. Just the immediate transition.

03:17.780 --> 03:22.400
So the rule only evaluates the keystroke I just made against the one I'm making right now.

03:22.520 --> 03:26.940
Exactly. And we can record those local rules in what's called a transition graph.

03:26.940 --> 03:28.260
A transition graph. Okay.

03:28.480 --> 03:37.700
Yeah. You map out the symbols as vertices and you draw directed arrows between them, but only if the rule allows that specific transition.

03:37.940 --> 03:43.560
So that single finite map governs every admissible sequence you could possibly generate.

03:43.680 --> 03:48.220
Right. Whether it's three characters long or, you know, three billion characters long.

03:48.220 --> 03:57.080
That is wild. And then the text translates this abstract idea of a transition graph into something measurable using an adjacency matrix.

03:57.440 --> 03:57.660
Yes.

03:57.900 --> 04:03.100
I want to spend a minute here because this is where the mechanics of the math get really, really elegant.

04:03.440 --> 04:04.640
It's a beautiful concept.

04:04.640 --> 04:09.880
So we all know an adjacency matrix is just a grid of numbers representing the connections in a graph.

04:10.540 --> 04:16.120
Like A1 means there's a valid path from node A to node B, and a 0 means there isn't.

04:16.180 --> 04:17.460
Right. Pretty standard stuff.

04:17.460 --> 04:27.300
But the text states that if you multiply this matrix by itself, the resulting numbers perfectly predict exactly how many admissible sequences exist of length 2.

04:27.700 --> 04:29.420
And you multiply it again, you get length 3.

04:29.800 --> 04:32.540
Yes. I want to unpack the how of that.

04:32.660 --> 04:38.280
Like, how does a grid of 1s and 0s become a prediction engine just by squaring it?

04:38.400 --> 04:40.820
Well, think about the mechanics of matrix multiplication.

04:40.820 --> 04:48.040
Let's say you want to find the value in the new squared matrix for row A, column C.

04:48.200 --> 04:48.420
Okay.

04:48.780 --> 04:53.200
That value represents the number of valid two-step paths from A to C.

04:53.560 --> 05:01.360
So to calculate it, you take the entire row A from your first matrix, and you compute the dot product with the entire column C of the second matrix.

05:01.480 --> 05:05.940
Right. So you are matching up the elements term by term, multiplying them, and adding the results.

05:05.940 --> 05:08.820
Which logically mirrors the act of tracing a path.

05:08.900 --> 05:09.940
Oh, wow. Yeah.

05:10.080 --> 05:16.300
You are asking, essentially, can I go from A to an intermediate node, say, node B? That's the first number.

05:16.380 --> 05:16.600
Right.

05:16.600 --> 05:21.620
And then you multiply it by, well, can I go from that intermediate node, B, to my final destination, C?

05:21.820 --> 05:24.340
So the 1s and 0s are acting as logical A and D gates.

05:24.480 --> 05:26.900
If the path from A to B is valid, that's a 1.

05:27.140 --> 05:29.940
If the path from B to C is valid, that's a 1.

05:30.360 --> 05:31.820
1 times 1 is 1.

05:31.900 --> 05:34.580
A valid two-step path survives the multiplication.

05:34.940 --> 05:41.040
But if either leg of the journey is invalid-like, a 0, the multiplication just kills the path.

05:41.360 --> 05:42.800
1 times 0 is 0.

05:42.940 --> 05:43.840
It's a dead end.

05:43.840 --> 05:56.700
Yep. And when you sum up all those resulting products across the row and column, you are literally just tallying up every surviving valid two-step route from A to C through any possible intermediate node.

05:56.800 --> 05:57.980
That is so elegant.

05:58.260 --> 05:58.700
It is.

05:59.400 --> 06:10.860
The text uses induction to prove this scales up to any power, but the deeper philosophical point the author is making is that the constraint itself generates a highly specific, countable architecture.

06:10.860 --> 06:13.660
The surviving subset of sequences isn't random.

06:13.840 --> 06:16.880
No, it inherits its entire topography from the rule that birthed it.

06:17.000 --> 06:21.580
The author grounds this whole idea in positional notation, like our base 10 numeric system.

06:21.600 --> 06:22.840
Right, the numbers we use every day.

06:23.060 --> 06:23.500
Exactly.

06:23.500 --> 06:35.820
We take whole numbers for granted, but the text frames a nine-digit number as a discrete state space governed by a single highly local constraint concentrated entirely on the very first position.

06:36.160 --> 06:38.180
Which is that the first digit cannot be 0.

06:38.180 --> 06:38.540
Right.

06:38.540 --> 06:50.500
Digits 2 through 9 can be anything 0 through 9, but that first one has a rule, so counting the total number of valid nine-digit sequences doesn't require us to, you know, count them one by one.

06:50.840 --> 06:52.460
The structure does the counting for you.

06:52.940 --> 06:58.300
You have nine choices for the first slot, multiplied by ten choices for each of the remaining eight slots.

06:58.440 --> 07:00.020
Nine times ten to the eighth power?

07:00.020 --> 07:00.700
Exactly.

07:01.120 --> 07:04.620
The constraint perfectly binds the edges of our numeric system.

07:05.060 --> 07:12.260
And speaking of numeric structures, the text includes these fascinating comparative notes that venture outside traditional Western math.

07:12.340 --> 07:13.460
Oh, I love these sections.

07:13.700 --> 07:18.900
Yeah, there's one on Cistercian numerals that beautifully illustrates this concept of composing constraints.

07:18.900 --> 07:21.140
Right, this is Cistercian monks in the Middle Ages.

07:21.680 --> 07:29.740
They developed this system to write any whole number from 1 to 9,999 as a single combined glyph.

07:30.060 --> 07:33.000
Just one symbol for a four-digit number.

07:33.640 --> 07:41.720
It's an incredibly efficient use of visual space, but the mathematical underpinnings are what make it relevant for our deep dive today.

07:41.720 --> 07:48.880
Yeah, the author draws a direct line between this 13th century monastic script and the concept of independent coordinates.

07:49.360 --> 07:52.780
So the system uses a single vertical stem as the base.

07:52.940 --> 07:53.080
Right.

07:53.340 --> 07:55.600
And on that stem, there are four fixed positions.

07:56.260 --> 08:02.900
There's the top right for units, the top left for tens, the bottom right for hundreds, and the bottom left for thousands.

08:03.500 --> 08:07.000
And each position can hold one of ten possible stroke patterns.

08:07.000 --> 08:14.480
So here is the crucial element. What you draw in the units position has zero effect on what you are allowed to draw in the tens position.

08:14.640 --> 08:19.240
The choices are structurally isolated from one another. They operate in independent dimensions.

08:19.360 --> 08:24.480
Because nothing constrains the adjacent choices, you compose the total space by multiplying.

08:24.700 --> 08:27.140
Ten choices times ten times ten times ten.

08:27.180 --> 08:31.000
You get 10,000 representable values, including the empty stem for zero.

08:31.480 --> 08:34.480
The monks essentially engineered a discrete vector space.

08:34.480 --> 08:44.300
They really did. They took a tiny, finite alphabet of ten strokes and mapped it across independent axes to multiply their expressive power exponentially.

08:44.860 --> 08:50.000
It demonstrates that complexity doesn't require a massive, unwieldy set of raw materials.

08:50.520 --> 08:55.640
It just requires a small set of constrained parts intercepting across independent dimensions.

08:56.000 --> 08:56.360
Exactly.

08:56.360 --> 09:01.880
So we established that rules create structure in discrete, finite spaces.

09:02.260 --> 09:07.720
You can count the strokes, you can tally the matrix paths, but the text pivots pretty sharply here.

09:07.860 --> 09:14.420
It asks, what happens when we apply this exact same philosophy to infinite, continuous space?

09:14.560 --> 09:14.760
Right.

09:14.760 --> 09:19.720
We leave behind the realm of counting symbols, and we enter the geometry of shapes, sizes, and scale.

09:20.160 --> 09:26.660
And the central question really becomes, when a continuous object undergoes a transformation, what actually survives?

09:26.840 --> 09:32.520
The text starts with this thought experiment regarding maps, which introduces scale transformations or dilations.

09:32.700 --> 09:34.140
Let's talk about that map example.

09:34.220 --> 09:40.500
Sure. So imagine you have a pocket map of a city, and then you have a massive wall-sized map of the exact same city.

09:40.500 --> 09:44.100
The absolute physical measurements on the paper are completely different.

09:44.900 --> 09:51.320
The area, the perimeter, the physical distance between two intersections, all of it is vastly larger on the wall map.

09:51.580 --> 09:53.140
But the shape of the city persists.

09:53.460 --> 09:57.740
The larger map doesn't invent new avenues, and the smaller map doesn't delete the river.

09:57.900 --> 10:04.780
Right. Mathematically, what survives is the ratio between the lengths of the streets and the angles at which they intersect.

10:04.780 --> 10:17.380
The text defines a dilation formally as a point in the Cartesian plane, x, y, is multiplied by a scale factor, k, moving to k times x, k times y.

10:17.600 --> 10:20.340
It changes magnitude, but preserves the proportional structure.

10:20.700 --> 10:25.440
Two figures are considered similar if they share this invariant shape, regardless of their absolute size.

10:25.440 --> 10:36.240
I really want to dig into this concept of invariance, because the text makes the bold claim that ratios are the absolute bedrock of geometry, specifically because they ignore size.

10:36.320 --> 10:37.200
You're immune to it.

10:37.340 --> 10:38.820
Let's look at the mechanism of that.

10:39.040 --> 10:44.960
Because if I take a digital photograph of a triangle and scale it up by 200%, the scale factor k is 2.

10:45.480 --> 10:48.500
Every coordinate has doubled. Every length is twice as long.

10:48.900 --> 10:52.520
To our senses, the entire object has been fundamentally altered.

10:52.620 --> 10:54.480
It feels like a completely different triangle.

10:54.480 --> 11:01.100
Exactly. So how does the ratio mathematically immunize itself against that scale factor?

11:01.520 --> 11:07.000
Well, the text provides a proof in Theorem 1.3 that is just elegantly simple.

11:07.860 --> 11:08.940
Let's trace the algebra.

11:09.280 --> 11:10.180
Okay, let's do it.

11:10.220 --> 11:12.500
You have two line segments in your original image.

11:13.020 --> 11:16.640
Segment A with length little a, and segment B with length little b.

11:16.760 --> 11:16.980
Right.

11:17.340 --> 11:19.400
The original ratio is a divided by b.

11:19.780 --> 11:22.300
Now you scale the image by a factor of k.

11:22.300 --> 11:25.140
The new lengths are k times a and k times b.

11:25.360 --> 11:28.620
So they are physically altered by the multiplier. They are larger now.

11:28.760 --> 11:29.060
Yes.

11:29.760 --> 11:34.460
But when you compute the ratio of those new lengths, you are dividing k times a by k times b.

11:34.620 --> 11:37.320
And assuming k is a non-zero scale factor?

11:37.380 --> 11:39.340
It exists in both the numerator and the denominator.

11:39.440 --> 11:41.780
Oh, so it algebraically cancels itself out.

11:42.080 --> 11:43.280
The k vanishes entirely.

11:43.280 --> 11:48.980
k times a divided by k times b simplifies instantly back to a divided by b.

11:49.900 --> 11:54.040
The ratio of the new lengths is mathematically identical to the ratio of the original lengths.

11:54.480 --> 11:57.960
The scale factor literally erases itself from the relationship.

11:58.400 --> 12:02.000
That algebraic cancellation is the mechanism of invariance.

12:02.120 --> 12:02.440
Exactly.

12:02.440 --> 12:04.760
The math self-corrects for the zoom function.

12:05.220 --> 12:09.560
It strips away the absolute magnitude and leaves only the structural relationship behind.

12:10.020 --> 12:13.380
And the text shows how this invariance is incredibly practical.

12:14.220 --> 12:18.560
We can weaponize it to measure the unmeasurable using the similarity criteria,

12:18.980 --> 12:20.620
like angle-angle, side-angle-side.

12:20.860 --> 12:25.920
The classic application is determining the height of an inaccessible object using shadows, right?

12:25.960 --> 12:26.920
That's a brilliant example.

12:26.960 --> 12:28.620
Like you want to measure a massive tree.

12:29.160 --> 12:31.140
You obviously can't climb it with a tape measure.

12:31.140 --> 12:31.780
Right.

12:32.100 --> 12:36.980
The text points out that because the sun is so distant, its rays arrive effectively parallel.

12:37.600 --> 12:41.880
This means the invisible triangle formed by the giant tree and its shadow on the ground

12:41.880 --> 12:48.540
is perfectly mathematically similar to the tiny triangle formed by a one-meter stick and its shadow.

12:48.940 --> 12:50.880
The angle-angle similarity holds.

12:51.340 --> 12:54.600
The sun's rays hit both objects at the exact same angle,

12:54.880 --> 12:59.260
and both the tree and the stick form a 90-degree angle with the flat ground.

12:59.260 --> 13:04.160
And because the triangles are similar, the ratios must survive the change in scale.

13:04.360 --> 13:04.960
They have to.

13:05.260 --> 13:10.900
So if the tree casts a 12-meter shadow and the one-meter stick casts a 0.8-meter shadow,

13:11.300 --> 13:12.800
you'd just set up the equivalence.

13:13.400 --> 13:19.480
The tree's unknown height, h, divided by 12, equals the stick's height, 1, divided by 0.8.

13:19.680 --> 13:23.740
Multiply both sides by 12, and you find the tree is exactly 15 meters tall.

13:23.740 --> 13:30.740
You extract a physical reality from the environment by relying on the fact that the ratio is immune to the tree's massive size.

13:31.440 --> 13:34.660
It's such a powerful illustration of why invariance matters,

13:35.380 --> 13:42.240
which connects to a brilliant philosophical note the author includes about why we bother naming things in science and mathematics.

13:42.580 --> 13:44.860
Yeah, we don't just assign terminology randomly.

13:45.100 --> 13:45.320
Right.

13:45.520 --> 13:51.660
The task argues that a quantity earns a name largely because it survives transformations that alter everything else around it.

13:51.660 --> 13:55.120
We name temperature because it survives the geometry of its container.

13:55.580 --> 13:55.780
Right.

13:56.280 --> 14:02.420
A cup of coffee has the same temperature whether you pour it into a tall, narrow thermos or a wide, shallow bowl.

14:02.860 --> 14:03.720
The shape changes.

14:03.980 --> 14:05.000
The temperature survives.

14:05.220 --> 14:08.760
And we name density because it survives the scale of the sample.

14:08.900 --> 14:09.100
Yeah.

14:09.220 --> 14:13.220
A tiny flick of gold and a massive brick of gold have identical density.

14:13.680 --> 14:18.180
The mass and volume change wildly, but the ratio, the density survives.

14:18.180 --> 14:21.640
We attach names to the anchors in a system.

14:22.500 --> 14:25.260
The properties that refuse to drift when the environment changes.

14:26.080 --> 14:29.560
That stability is what makes a concept universally applicable.

14:30.060 --> 14:32.480
And this is the foundational setup for trigonometry.

14:33.320 --> 14:38.660
A trigonometric ratio is given a name sign, cosine, tangent for one specific reason.

14:38.740 --> 14:41.960
Because it survives the scale of the right triangle it was computed from.

14:41.960 --> 14:48.440
Exactly. The author really wants us to stop viewing sign as just an arbitrary formula to memorize for a test.

14:48.580 --> 14:49.960
It's not a formula. It's a title.

14:50.140 --> 14:53.260
An honored title we give to an unkillable invariant structure.

14:53.520 --> 14:55.260
It's the standard maneuver in mathematics.

14:55.940 --> 15:01.860
You identify a family of transformations, you find the property that refuses to change during that transformation, and you name it.

15:02.080 --> 15:05.280
But the text points out that scale is only half the battle.

15:05.620 --> 15:05.820
Right.

15:05.820 --> 15:12.900
A map gives you the invariant shape of the city, but if you actually want to navigate through it, scale alone isn't enough.

15:13.240 --> 15:15.620
You need to understand direction and movement.

15:15.920 --> 15:19.060
We have to move from static geometry to the mathematics of displacement.

15:19.460 --> 15:21.040
Which requires a coordinate system.

15:21.780 --> 15:27.360
The text credits Rene Descartes and his 1637 appendix, La Geometry.

15:28.020 --> 15:31.560
He translated geometric shapes into algebraic relationships.

15:31.560 --> 15:39.000
Though the text is careful to note that Descartes didn't actually use the clean perpendicular intersecting axes we use today.

15:39.200 --> 15:39.700
Oh, really?

15:39.820 --> 15:43.260
Yeah, he used a single reference line and measured a second distance obliquely.

15:43.360 --> 15:43.720
Huh.

15:44.020 --> 15:50.880
The perpendicular axes were formalized by later mathematicians, but the core insight that space could be quantified algebraically.

15:51.100 --> 15:51.740
That was his.

15:52.200 --> 15:55.040
And from that Cartesian plane, we get the concept of vectors.

15:55.400 --> 15:55.840
Yes.

15:55.840 --> 16:01.880
The text defines a vector as an object possessing magnitude and direction, but lacking a fixed location.

16:02.340 --> 16:05.440
Which is a tough concept for some people to wrap their head around initially.

16:05.840 --> 16:06.700
Yeah, it is.

16:06.900 --> 16:17.860
But if you walk three kilometers east and two kilometers north, that displacement vector 3, comma 2 is the exact same mathematical object whether you started in New York or Tokyo.

16:18.060 --> 16:20.800
It's an instruction for movement, independent of origin.

16:20.800 --> 16:25.240
And because it lacks a fixed location, it can be manipulated purely algebraically.

16:25.640 --> 16:29.020
You can add vectors by summing their horizontal and vertical components.

16:29.200 --> 16:32.360
You can scale them by multiplying by a constant.

16:32.620 --> 16:39.120
Which is just applying the dilation transformation we discussed earlier, but to a displacement rather than a static shape.

16:39.240 --> 16:39.680
Exactly.

16:39.680 --> 16:43.300
But the crucial maneuver in this section of the text is decomposition.

16:43.880 --> 16:43.960
Yeah.

16:44.200 --> 16:53.680
When you take a diagonal vector and break it down into a purely horizontal x component and a purely vertical y component, you create an inescapable geometric reality.

16:54.580 --> 16:59.700
Every single vector in a Cartesian plane inherently carries a right triangle inside of it.

16:59.780 --> 17:01.740
The horizontal displacement is the bottom leg.

17:01.900 --> 17:03.780
The vertical displacement is the side leg.

17:03.900 --> 17:08.620
And the vector itself, the direct path from start to finish, is the hypotenuse.

17:08.620 --> 17:16.400
And because it is permanently a right triangle, we evaluate the magnitude of the vector using the Pythagorean theorem.

17:16.600 --> 17:16.860
Right.

17:17.140 --> 17:23.000
The length of vector x, comma y is the square root of x squared plus y squared.

17:23.180 --> 17:25.920
But measuring the magnitude only gives us the distance.

17:26.540 --> 17:33.220
A vector of length, 130, could be pointing in any direction across a 360-degree spread.

17:33.360 --> 17:33.620
Right.

17:33.780 --> 17:36.280
So how do we formally define the direction?

17:36.280 --> 17:38.140
This is where the concepts collide.

17:38.620 --> 17:46.300
We take the invariant ratios from the first chapter and we apply them to the inescapable right triangles hiding inside the vectors of the second chapter.

17:46.460 --> 17:47.180
It all comes together.

17:47.360 --> 17:55.000
Because the vector forms a right triangle with the axes, the angle of its direction can be perfectly defined by the ratios of the triangle sides.

17:55.000 --> 18:05.100
And as we proved earlier, because those ratios are invariant under scaling, the direction defined by the ratio remains constant, regardless of the vector's magnitude.

18:05.100 --> 18:08.720
The ratio of the opposite side over the hypotenuse is sine.

18:08.720 --> 18:11.780
The adjacent side over the hypotenuse is cosine.

18:11.780 --> 18:20.120
The text emphasizes that sine and cosine don't care if the vector represents a displacement of a few millimeters or a light year.

18:20.120 --> 18:22.460
The ratio defining the angle remains absolute.

18:22.840 --> 18:23.080
Yeah.

18:23.880 --> 18:27.040
But, okay, I have to stop here because we need to talk about the names themselves.

18:27.720 --> 18:34.680
The author includes a historical note on the etymology of the word sine, and it is an unbelievable story of translation errors.

18:34.780 --> 18:35.740
Oh, it's so fun.

18:35.740 --> 18:44.040
We are dealing with pristine universal mathematical truths, and the label we slacked on it is the result of a centuries-long game of telephone.

18:44.040 --> 18:52.400
It perfectly highlights the stark contrast between the objective reality of the math and the messy human reality of history.

18:52.600 --> 18:53.300
It really does.

18:53.440 --> 18:55.740
So, it starts in India in the first millennium.

18:56.100 --> 19:02.640
Indian astronomers are working with these ratios inside circles, and they use the term ja'arda, which is Sanskrit for half-cord.

19:02.640 --> 19:05.920
Which is a perfectly logical, descriptive, geometric term.

19:06.120 --> 19:07.200
Yes, very accurate.

19:07.680 --> 19:10.880
Over time, they abbreviate it to jarar or jivaat.

19:10.940 --> 19:13.080
Still functioning properly as a mathematical label.

19:13.200 --> 19:16.540
Then, Arabic scholars translate the Sanskrit texts.

19:17.040 --> 19:22.540
But instead of translating the meaning of the word half-cord, they just transliterate the phonetic sound.

19:22.680 --> 19:22.880
Right.

19:23.060 --> 19:24.540
So, jivaat becomes jiba.

19:25.260 --> 19:28.220
But jiba has absolutely no meaning in Arabic.

19:28.420 --> 19:30.880
It's a nonsense word, just a phonetic placeholder.

19:30.880 --> 19:33.440
And Arabic is typically written without vowels.

19:34.160 --> 19:36.760
The text only records the consonants for j and b.

19:37.000 --> 19:42.380
So, later readers come along, they see these consonants, and they try to read it as a legitimate Arabic word.

19:42.820 --> 19:46.880
They misread the phonetic placeholder jiba as the common word jiba.

19:47.020 --> 19:51.660
And jiba translates to a bay, a gulf, or a fold in a garment.

19:51.780 --> 19:53.000
Like the pocket of a shirt.

19:53.200 --> 19:56.100
We have drifted entirely away from geometry at this point.

19:56.180 --> 19:56.620
Completely.

19:57.080 --> 19:58.820
But the chain of errors continues.

19:58.820 --> 20:07.420
Fast forward to the 12th century, and Girard of Cremona is translating these Arabic texts into Latin for European scholars.

20:07.580 --> 20:08.540
And he sees the word jiba.

20:08.860 --> 20:09.040
Right.

20:09.300 --> 20:13.520
And instead of transliterating the sound, he translates the literal meaning of the word into Latin.

20:13.920 --> 20:17.840
The Latin word for a bay, a curve, or a fold of cloth is sinus.

20:17.840 --> 20:20.180
And from sinus, English inherits sinning.

20:20.380 --> 20:21.640
It's totally wild.

20:21.840 --> 20:32.200
The foundational function of trigonometry, the math used to map the stars, engineer bridges, and measure the frequencies of light and sound, is named after a fold in a shirt.

20:32.200 --> 20:41.800
All because a 12th century translator accurately translated a misread vowel on a meaningless phonetic abbreviation of a Sanskrit word.

20:41.800 --> 20:43.120
It just cracks me up.

20:43.320 --> 20:49.440
The author notes that cosine and cotangent manage to survive with slightly more logical names, at least.

20:49.520 --> 20:50.120
Oh, really?

20:50.400 --> 20:50.540
Yeah.

20:50.620 --> 20:57.440
The prefix co is just an abbreviation of complementary, noting that the cosine of an angle is equal to the sine of its complementary angle.

20:57.520 --> 20:57.760
Okay.

20:57.840 --> 20:58.740
That makes a bit more sense.

20:58.840 --> 20:58.940
Yeah.

20:58.940 --> 21:05.060
So, we have our weirdly named invariant ratios, and we can solve right triangles.

21:05.160 --> 21:07.960
We can calculate heights, distances, components of force.

21:08.360 --> 21:09.540
The math works perfectly.

21:09.980 --> 21:10.260
What?

21:10.500 --> 21:15.280
Yeah, but the text points out a catastrophic flaw in the system we've built.

21:15.400 --> 21:16.420
The trapped angle problem.

21:16.520 --> 21:16.780
Yes.

21:16.980 --> 21:27.040
If sine and cosine are strictly defined as the ratios of the physical sides inside a right triangle, then the angle we are measuring is physically trapped by the geometry of the polygon.

21:27.540 --> 21:27.780
Right.

21:27.780 --> 21:34.380
An acute angle inside a right triangle must be greater than zero, and strictly less than 90 degrees.

21:34.740 --> 21:38.140
If the angle hits 90 degrees, the two legs become parallel.

21:38.860 --> 21:41.380
The hypotenuse never intersects the adjacent side.

21:41.700 --> 21:43.780
The triangle literally ceases to exist.

21:43.840 --> 21:45.540
It completely shatters the model.

21:45.740 --> 21:47.920
You cannot have a negative interior angle.

21:48.600 --> 21:54.180
And what happens if I want to describe the rotation of an engine crank turning 720 degrees?

21:54.280 --> 21:56.580
Right triangle trigonometry is entirely blind to it.

21:56.580 --> 22:02.820
Our tool for describing direction breaks the second the direction points backwards, or spins in a circle.

22:03.180 --> 22:04.320
We need a new constraint.

22:04.320 --> 22:07.700
And this is where the text pivots from the polygon to the continuous curve.

22:08.100 --> 22:13.340
We abandon the triangle, and we constrain the entire Cartesian plane to a single equation.

22:13.640 --> 22:16.340
X squared plus Y squared equals 1.

22:16.500 --> 22:17.200
The unit circle.

22:17.420 --> 22:19.940
This is a profound shift in perspective.

22:20.140 --> 22:20.460
It is.

22:20.980 --> 22:25.160
The unit circle is a discrete state space applied to continuous reality.

22:25.160 --> 22:28.260
The entire infinite plane of coordinates is the alphabet.

22:28.620 --> 22:28.680
Right.

22:28.900 --> 22:33.540
The equation X squared plus Y squared equals 1 is the admissibility constraint.

22:34.180 --> 22:39.860
Only points that satisfy that exact relationship are allowed to exist in this new space.

22:40.080 --> 22:43.620
And think about what that constraint actually accomplishes mathematically.

22:43.620 --> 22:48.720
By forcing the radius to always equal exactly 1, you isolate the variance.

22:49.000 --> 22:49.780
Size is frozen.

22:50.180 --> 22:51.220
Magnitude is locked.

22:51.540 --> 22:54.860
It's analogous to normalizing a data set in computer science.

22:54.900 --> 22:55.860
Oh, that's a good comparison.

22:56.120 --> 22:56.260
Yeah.

22:56.320 --> 23:01.380
If you have a data set measuring two variables, but one variable is in the billions and the other is in the decimals,

23:01.960 --> 23:04.100
the absolute volume drowns out the pattern.

23:04.220 --> 23:05.460
You can't see the relationship.

23:05.660 --> 23:05.880
Right.

23:06.060 --> 23:07.080
You normalize the data.

23:07.260 --> 23:07.480
Yeah.

23:07.620 --> 23:10.080
You force the baseline to a standard scale.

23:10.300 --> 23:13.900
So the algorithm only judges the variance, the underlying relationship.

23:14.100 --> 23:16.600
And the unit circle normalizes geometry.

23:16.940 --> 23:17.360
Exactly.

23:17.600 --> 23:20.560
It strips away magnitude so we can isolate and study pure direction.

23:20.920 --> 23:26.000
So under this new constraint, an angle is no longer the interior corner of a shape.

23:26.260 --> 23:28.100
It is redefined as a rotation.

23:28.100 --> 23:30.760
You start at the positive X axis and you rotate.

23:31.840 --> 23:32.960
Counterclockwise is positive.

23:33.140 --> 23:34.060
Clockwise is negative.

23:34.100 --> 23:40.280
Where your rotated line intersects the perimeter of the unit circle is your coordinate point, X comma Y.

23:40.500 --> 23:43.220
And here, the text delivers the ultimate redefinition.

23:43.780 --> 23:49.400
For any rotation angle theta, cosine is simply defined as the X coordinate of that point on the circle.

23:49.500 --> 23:51.520
And sine is defined as the Y coordinate.

23:51.820 --> 23:52.200
Exactly.

23:52.280 --> 23:53.380
No opposite sides.

23:53.620 --> 23:54.620
No hypotenuse.

23:55.100 --> 23:57.160
Sine is just the vertical height on the graph.

23:57.640 --> 23:59.700
Cosine is just the horizontal distance.

23:59.860 --> 24:03.140
And because they are just coordinates on a grid, the cage is open.

24:03.140 --> 24:11.480
We can rotate 150 degrees into the second quadrant where X is negative and Y is positive, giving us a negative cosine and a positive sine.

24:11.540 --> 24:13.860
We can rotate negative 45 degrees.

24:14.020 --> 24:16.320
We can spin around the circle 100 times.

24:16.460 --> 24:19.960
And the text proves that this doesn't invalidate the old right triangle math.

24:19.960 --> 24:20.920
No, not at all.

24:20.920 --> 24:29.560
If you draw a right triangle inside the unit circle, dropping a line from the point straight down to the X axis, the hypotenuse is the radius, which is 1.

24:29.560 --> 24:35.300
So the old ratio opposite over hypotenuse becomes Y over 1, which is just Y.

24:35.700 --> 24:40.780
The new definition perfectly encompasses the old one while expanding its domain to infinity.

24:41.160 --> 24:45.440
This expansion introduces periodicity, coterminal angles.

24:45.600 --> 24:45.780
Right.

24:45.780 --> 24:57.900
Because we are trapped on the perimeter of a circle, if you start at an angle and add 360 degrees, you complete a full revolution and land on the exact same X comma Y coordinate.

24:58.120 --> 25:06.060
40 degrees and 400 degrees represent completely different amounts of physical rotation, but they share the exact same structural location.

25:06.340 --> 25:08.640
They have identical signs and cosines.

25:08.640 --> 25:12.900
The output of the function repeats in an infinite, predictable loop.

25:13.220 --> 25:18.600
But before we get too deep into that looping motion, I want to address a structural shift the text demands.

25:19.040 --> 25:19.600
Degrees.

25:19.840 --> 25:21.200
Degrees versus radians.

25:21.360 --> 25:21.660
Yes.

25:22.140 --> 25:31.240
360 degrees in a circle feels incredibly natural to human intuition, likely tracing back to ancient approximations of the 365-day solar year.

25:31.360 --> 25:32.700
Yeah, it's very culturally ingrained.

25:32.700 --> 25:39.160
But the text, and mathematicians in general, insist on using radians, and radians feel deeply unintuitive.

25:39.680 --> 25:43.520
A full circle is 2 pi radians, roughly 6.28.

25:43.800 --> 25:44.020
Right.

25:44.400 --> 25:47.820
Why abandon a clean integer system for an irrational number?

25:48.180 --> 25:50.020
Well, degrees are a cultural artifact.

25:50.540 --> 25:53.060
They are a human convention mapped onto geometry.

25:53.700 --> 25:58.980
But the text argues that radians are the inherent structural language of the circle itself.

25:59.160 --> 26:02.340
Okay, let's look at the mechanical definition of a radian.

26:02.340 --> 26:10.920
One radian is defined as the angle that carves out an arc on the circumference of the circle whose physical length is exactly equal to the length of the circle's radius.

26:11.200 --> 26:20.040
So if the radius of the circle is 7 centimeters, one radian is the angle where the curved arc length along the edge is exactly 7 centimeters.

26:20.140 --> 26:20.320
Right.

26:20.720 --> 26:30.920
And because the total circumference of any circle is 2 times pi times the radius 2 pi r, it naturally follows that a full revolution contains exactly 2 pi radians.

26:30.920 --> 26:32.120
It's conversion is direct.

26:32.720 --> 26:35.620
180 degrees equals pi radians.

26:35.880 --> 26:38.720
But why is that structural language superior for mathematics?

26:39.340 --> 26:40.560
Yeah, that's the question.

26:40.780 --> 26:47.320
The superiority reveals itself when you try to calculate the distance traveled along the arc length, usually denoted as s.

26:47.320 --> 26:55.260
Based on the definition we just established, an angle measured in radians theta is literally the ratio of the arc length to the radius.

26:55.840 --> 26:57.420
Theta equals s divided by r.

26:57.520 --> 27:03.080
Which means if you rearrange the algebra, multiplying both sides by r, you get s equals r times theta.

27:03.860 --> 27:06.420
Arc length equals the radius multiplied by the angle.

27:06.620 --> 27:09.360
It is a pristine, structurally perfect equation.

27:09.360 --> 27:12.600
And if you attempt to calculate arc length using degrees.

27:12.820 --> 27:22.360
The formula requires an ugly conversion factor, multiplying by pi over 180 just to force the arbitrary human units to balance with the geometric reality.

27:22.520 --> 27:24.780
Radiants strip away the human bias.

27:24.920 --> 27:28.380
They allow the radius and the circumference to speak directly to each other.

27:28.560 --> 27:30.600
The math just clicks together without friction.

27:30.600 --> 27:40.360
And this natural cyclical structure of things repeating in predictable loops brings us to a fantastic comparative note in the text regarding the Chinese sixaginary cycle.

27:40.540 --> 27:41.400
The calendar system.

27:41.480 --> 27:41.660
Yeah.

27:41.900 --> 27:46.740
The author uses it to illustrate how periodicity governs systems far beyond geometry.

27:47.300 --> 27:50.980
It's a really compelling look at modular arithmetic applied to timekeeping.

27:51.320 --> 27:54.760
Traditional Chinese calendars utilize this cycle to track years.

27:55.300 --> 27:58.420
The system relies on pairing two distinct sets of symbols.

27:58.420 --> 28:03.960
You have 10 symbols known as the heavenly stems and 12 symbols known as the earthly branches.

28:04.560 --> 28:09.600
Think of them as two interlocking gears of different sizes turning simultaneously.

28:09.960 --> 28:10.340
Exactly.

28:10.840 --> 28:12.760
Year one is stem one and branch one.

28:13.220 --> 28:15.260
Year two is stem two and branch two.

28:15.420 --> 28:16.760
They advance in lockstep.

28:16.840 --> 28:17.040
Right.

28:17.280 --> 28:23.880
Now, basic intuition might suggest that to find the total number of unique combinations, you just multiply the sizes of the two sets.

28:24.180 --> 28:25.660
10 times 12 is 120.

28:25.660 --> 28:32.600
But the constraint of the system, the fact that they advance simultaneously, prevents certain pairings from ever occurring.

28:32.800 --> 28:33.020
Right.

28:33.440 --> 28:35.520
Stem one will never align with branch two.

28:35.820 --> 28:36.180
Never.

28:36.480 --> 28:43.640
So to find out when the cycle actually resets, when it returns to stem one and branch one simultaneously, you don't multiply.

28:43.800 --> 28:47.100
You have to find the least common multiple of 10 and 12.

28:47.100 --> 28:47.920
Which is 60.

28:48.120 --> 28:50.240
The cycle resets every 60 steps.

28:50.580 --> 28:51.800
The 60-year calendar.

28:52.600 --> 28:56.820
The author highlights this to show that periodicity is a universal structural phenomenon.

28:57.320 --> 29:03.600
Two distinct periods interacting will create a new, larger period governed by their common multiples.

29:03.920 --> 29:12.100
It's modular arithmetic playing out in the organization of human institutions, centuries before Western mathematics formalized the theory of cyclic groups.

29:12.100 --> 29:14.560
And the parallel to trigonometry is direct.

29:15.380 --> 29:17.400
The unit circle is entirely modular.

29:18.260 --> 29:23.480
The terminal angles are just values that differ by a multiple of the circle's fundamental period, 2 pi.

29:23.660 --> 29:23.880
Right.

29:24.240 --> 29:31.000
When you begin adding different oscillating sine waves together, which the text points out as the foundation of Fourier analysis later on,

29:31.000 --> 29:38.160
the interacting frequencies create complex, combined periods governed by those exact same mathematical rules.

29:38.480 --> 29:46.300
Whether you are tracking a 60-year calendar, analyzing the harmonics of a vibrating guitar string, or predicting the orbital alignments of the planets,

29:46.940 --> 29:50.260
it is all cyclical movement governed by structural constraints.

29:50.540 --> 29:53.980
Which brings us to the final horizon of this text, the introduction of time.

29:54.280 --> 29:54.560
Yes.

29:55.040 --> 29:58.280
Up until this point, even on the unit circle, our angles have been static.

29:58.280 --> 30:02.220
We rotated 150 degrees and stopped to measure the coordinates.

30:02.640 --> 30:03.920
But reality doesn't stop.

30:04.500 --> 30:06.720
The universe is in constant motion.

30:07.220 --> 30:09.480
The angle theta is no longer a fixed value.

30:09.820 --> 30:12.120
It becomes a function of time, theta of t.

30:12.360 --> 30:16.540
And once your angle is continuously changing over time, you can measure the rate of that change.

30:16.640 --> 30:17.960
You derive angular velocity.

30:18.220 --> 30:21.300
This is where the entire architecture of the text fulfills its purpose.

30:21.580 --> 30:21.840
Yes.

30:22.440 --> 30:27.100
The discrete rules that built the matrices, the scale and variances that define the ratios,

30:27.100 --> 30:33.180
the right triangles hidden inside the vectors, the unit circle normalizing the plane to isolate direction.

30:33.700 --> 30:38.280
All of these are the necessary grammatical components required to write the language of dynamic motion.

30:38.520 --> 30:46.240
The text evolves trigonometry from a static tool for solving triangles into a unified theory of continuous periodic reality.

30:46.240 --> 30:49.120
It fundamentally changes how you perceive the world around you.

30:49.460 --> 30:52.900
And that brings me to a final thought I want to leave with you, the listener.

30:53.260 --> 30:55.780
Something to consider long after this discussion ends.

30:56.040 --> 30:59.900
We started this journey by acknowledging our inherent bias against rules.

31:00.300 --> 31:03.500
We view constraints as cages that limit our potential.

31:04.220 --> 31:07.900
But throughout this text, the mathematics have proven the exact opposite.

31:08.160 --> 31:09.020
They really have.

31:09.020 --> 31:16.640
It is the constraint, the strict limitation that actually births the structure, the meaning and the utility of the system.

31:17.040 --> 31:25.660
Without the constraint, without the transition rules pruning the sequences, without the unit circle violently restricting the radius to exactly one,

31:26.040 --> 31:30.180
you are left with a chaotic, meaningless soup of infinite possibilities.

31:30.180 --> 31:34.320
Pure freedom, in a mathematical sense, is just noise.

31:34.460 --> 31:39.640
Exactly. It cannot be navigated, it cannot be measured, and it cannot build anything sustainable.

31:40.100 --> 31:45.640
So look at the architecture of your own life, your work, your relationships, your creative projects, your business strategies.

31:46.120 --> 31:49.020
We spend so much energy fighting against our limitations.

31:49.400 --> 31:55.420
We constantly wish for infinite resources, an unlimited budget, total unconstrained freedom, more hours in the day.

31:55.500 --> 31:59.540
We are convinced that if the cage were removed, we could finally achieve our vision.

31:59.540 --> 32:03.020
But what if you are misinterpreting the mechanics of your situation?

32:03.560 --> 32:08.620
What if the specific constraints you are wrestling with right now, the impossibly tight deadline, the shrinking budget,

32:08.760 --> 32:14.720
the physical or geographic limitations you are stuck within, are not obstacles blocking your path?

32:15.020 --> 32:20.700
What if they are the exact mathematical parameters required to generate your unique admissible structure?

32:20.960 --> 32:26.960
What if the cage you are trying to break out of is actually the skeleton that will finally allow your work to stand up?

32:27.300 --> 32:28.960
Thank you for exploring this with us.

32:28.960 --> 32:29.900
Until next time!

